# Working on @CHECKER@

## The loop

```bash
install/get-eo-compiler.sh          # once
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh      # signature -> Lean
scripts/build.sh                    # check the result
```

After a change to the signature or its semantics, install and build again.
Regeneration overwrites the signature-wide modules and **preserves** everything
under `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Rules/`, so proofs are never lost — see
[`install/README.md`](../install/README.md).

## What to build, and what not to

`scripts/build.sh` builds the library and the executable. It does **not** build
the correctness proof: `@CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect` depends on every per-rule proof,
which is far too slow for a routine build. Ask for it explicitly, and expect it
to take a long time:

```bash
scripts/build.sh @CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect
```

While working on one rule, build just that rule:

```bash
scripts/build.sh @CALCULUS@.Proofs.Rules.Symm
```

## Checking without Lean

`install/get-eo-compiler.sh` builds ethos alongside the compiler. Ethos reads
`install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo` directly and checks proofs against it, so it works
before any of this development does:

```bash
scripts/check-with-ethos.sh
```

While a signature is being written, that is the fast loop: edit the `.eo`, check
a proof, repeat, with no Lean build and nothing generated. Afterwards it stays
useful as a second opinion — `scripts/run-ci.sh` runs it as the `ethos` group,
comparing verdicts against @EXE@'s.

Ethos is unverified. Agreement with it proves nothing; disagreement is a strong
signal that the compiled calculus and the signature have parted company.

## Checks

```bash
scripts/run-ci.sh              # everything CI runs
scripts/run-ci.sh hygiene      # just the sorry/admit/axiom check
```

`scripts/check-proof-hygiene.sh` is textual and builds nothing. That is what
makes it worth running constantly: a full proof build is too slow to gate every
change, so without it an unproven rule could land unnoticed.

### Future work: turn proof hygiene on

It is **not** run by CI yet, and not in the default groups of
`scripts/run-ci.sh`. A freshly generated checker has one `sorry` per rule of the
signature, plus the soundness theorem in `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Checker.lean`, so
enabling it now would make every build red — and a CI that is red by
construction is one nobody reads.

Meanwhile it is the work list:

```bash
scripts/run-ci.sh hygiene      # what is still unproven
```

Turn it on for good once that list is empty:

1. uncomment the `Proof hygiene` step in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`;
2. add `hygiene` to the default groups in `scripts/run-ci.sh`.

Do it as soon as the list *is* empty rather than later: the value of the check
is stopping the first unproven rule from landing, which it can only do if it is
already running.

`install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh --check` reports whether `@CALCULUS@/` has
drifted from the signature it was compiled from, writing nothing.

## Where the correctness argument lives

| file | what it establishes |
| ---- | ------------------- |
| `@CALCULUS@/SmtModel.lean` | the model semantics of SMT-LIB, on its own terms |
| `@CALCULUS@/Spec.lean` | the correspondence between @CALCULUS@ terms and SMT-LIB, and satisfiability |
| `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Assumptions.lean` | the side conditions, and the instances that decide them |
| `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/CheckerCore.lean` | correctness of the core checker, independent of the rules |
| `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Rules/*.lean` | one correctness proof per rule |
| `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/RuleLemmas.lean` | the dispatcher tying the rules to the core |
| `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Checker.lean` | the soundness theorem |
| `@CALCULUS@/ApiCorrect.lean` | that theorem, restated about the text of a proof file |

The side conditions are checked at run time, which is why a proof the checker
accepts can still come back `incomplete`: it mentions something the semantics
does not model, so the theorem does not apply to it.

## The proofs

The [Logos experience report](https://github.com/ajreynol/eudaimonia/blob/main/docs/logos-experience-report.md)
goes through every `sorry` this checker contains, in reading order, with what
the same obligation cost Logos. It is in the Eudaimonia repository, since it
describes Logos rather than @CALCULUS@. Start with `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Invariants/Extra.lean` — the one file
that genuinely differs between two checkers, and the decision that most affects
the rest.

## The rule proofs

This is the bulk of the work, and the part no tooling can do for you. Two
scripts exist to keep it measurable and to keep a full build survivable.

```bash
scripts/rule-status.sh          # how many are proven, and what is left
scripts/build-rules.sh          # build the proofs that exist
```

`rule-status.sh` is textual and instant. It counts what is *in the files*, not
what has been built, so a proof counts as soon as it is written.

`build-rules.sh` builds one rule per Lake invocation by default. That is
deliberate: several Lean processes on proofs this size can exhaust memory, and
Lake caches what completes, so an interrupted run resumes rather than starting
over. `--batch-size N` trades memory for speed.

It builds only rules that are **actually proven**. A rule still holding a
`sorry` cannot build at all — this package builds with warnings as errors — so
attempting them would bury the real question under hundreds of identical
failures. The real question is the other one: *of the proofs that exist, which
still hold?*

That is the question to ask after regenerating the calculus, and it is why a
full run matters:

```bash
scripts/build-rules.sh --keep-going
```

**Expect hours** once the rules are written. It is not something to put in CI.
Run it before a release, or after a regeneration.

### The reduced package

When this project was generated with `--mini` it also carries `@MINI@` — the
same signature compiled with a handful of rules and no parser.

```bash
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh --mini    # refresh it from the signature
scripts/build.sh @MINI@                  # seconds, not minutes
```

Measured on the CPC example: **8 seconds against 83**, 2,130 lines against
20,350, five rule files against 591. That ratio is the entire point. A proof
about the *checker* — an invariant, a preservation lemma, the soundness
argument — does not depend on how many rules the calculus has, so develop it
here and move it over once it holds.

It is not a different calculus. Same signature, same semantics, fewer rules,
which is what makes a proof developed against it worth moving. Logos reports
the payoff bluntly: its `Proofs/Checker.lean` is byte-identical between the full
and reduced packages, and the two differ in rule set, signature *and* required
invariants — so a proof holding for both did not quietly depend on the calculus.

Which rules it keeps is set by `MINI_RULES` near the top of
`install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh`. Pick ones that exercise the *checker* rather
than the theories: something that discharges an assumption, a transitivity, a
contradiction.

### Before any of that works

`@CALCULUS@/Proofs/RuleSupport/Support.lean` is the module every generated rule
statement imports, and it is a stub. Until it supplies `StepRuleProperties`,
`premiseTermList`, `model_wf` and the rest, no rule can be built — not because
its proof is missing, but because there is nothing to state it against.
`build-rules.sh` says so rather than reporting several hundred identical
errors.

## Adding a rule

1. Add it to `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo`.
2. `install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh` — it arrives as a `sorry` stub in
   `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Rules/`.
3. Discharge the stub.
4. `scripts/build-rules.sh <Rule>` — a named rule is built whether or not it is
   still a stub, so this is the loop while working on one.

Changing an existing rule's *statement* leaves its old proof in place, so the
build fails. That is the intended signal, not a fault in the install.
